      "Language": "fre",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "PURPOSE OF THE STUDY",
          "content": "We describe a case of acute volar compartment syndrome of the forearm occurred after fracture of the diaphyseal-metaphyseal junction of the distal radius and ulna."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MATERIAL",
          "content": "A 7-year-old child fell down steps and injured his left forearm."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "After reduction, the patient developed clinical signs of volar forearm compartment syndrome. Measurement of intramuscular pressure by the saline injection technique confirmed the diagnosis."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Early decompressive fasciotomy led to uneventful healing with no residual symptoms."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "DISCUSSION",
          "content": "We discuss the mechanism, diagnosis and treatment of this injury."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "The development of suspicious clinical signs should be supplemented by compartment pressure measurements to determine the need of surgical decompression."
        }
      ]},
